# Configuration file for dircolors, a utility to help you set the
# LS_COLORS environment variable used by GNU ls with the --color option.
# Copyright (C) 1996-2016 Free Software Foundation, Inc.
# Copying and distribution of this file, with or without modification,
# are permitted provided the copyright notice and this notice are preserved.
# The keywords COLOR, OPTIONS, and EIGHTBIT (honored by the
# slackware version of dircolors) are recognized but ignored.
# Below are TERM entries, which can be a glob patterns, to match
# against the TERM environment variable to determine if it is colorizable.
TERM	Eterm
TERM	ansi
TERM	*color*
TERM	*colour*
TERM	con[0-9]*x[0-9]*
TERM	cons25
TERM	console
TERM	cygwin
TERM	dtterm
TERM	gnome
TERM	gnome-256color
TERM	hurd
TERM	jfbterm
TERM	konsole
TERM	kterm
TERM	linux
TERM	linux-c
TERM	mlterm
TERM	putty
TERM	putty-256color
TERM	rxvt*
TERM	screen*
TERM	st
TERM	st-256color
TERM	terminator
TERM	tmux*
TERM	vt???*
TERM	xterm*

# Below are the colour init strings for the basic file types.
# Each colour init string consists of one or more of the following numeric
# codes, separated by semicolons:
#
# Attribute codes:
#      0←reset everything, approximately equivalent to 22;23;24;25;26;27;28;29;39;49
#      (0 is implied by an empty attribute list)
#
#      1←bold  2←feint   22←normal weight
#      3←italic/oblique  23←roman
#      4←underscored     24←not underscored
#      5←blinking        25←not blinking
#     (6←blinking only on Xterm; 26 does not cancel blinking)
#      7←swap fg & bg    27←unswapped
#      8←concealed       28←revealed (uses background colour as foreground)
#      9←strike-through  29←no strike-through
#
# Colour codes are based on primary color bits: 1=red, 2=green, 4=blue:
#   foreground   light	 30←black    31←red      32←green    33←yellow   34←blue     35←magenta  36←cyan     37←white
#                 dark	 90←black    91←red      92←green    93←yellow   94←blue     95←magenta  96←cyan     97←white
#           24-bit RGB	 38;2;R;G;B
#        8-bit palette	 38;5;X
#              default	 39
#
#   background   light	 40←black    41←red      42←green    43←yellow   44←blue     45←magenta  46←cyan     47←white
#                 dark	100←black   101←red     102←green   103←yellow  104←blue    105←magenta 106←cyan    107←white
#           24-bit RGB	 48;2;R;G;B
#        8-bit palette	 48;5;X
#              default	 49

#NORMAL	0	# no colour code at all
#FILE	0	# regular file: use no colour at all
RESET	0	# reset to "normal" color
DIR	1;38;5;39	# directory
LINK	36	# symbolic link. (If you set this to 'target' instead of a numerical value, the color is the same as the file pointed to.)
MULTIHARDLINK	1	# regular file with more than one link
FIFO	33	# pipe
SOCK	44;35	# socket
DOOR	44;35	# door
BLK	44;33	# block device driver
CHR	44;33	# character device driver
ORPHAN	3;36	# symlink to nonexistent file, or non-stat'able file (italic) ...
MISSING	9;31	# ... and the files they point to (strikethrough)
SETUID	37;41	# file that is setuid (u+s)
SETGID	30;43	# file that is setgid (g+s)
CAPABILITY	30;41	# file with capability
STICKY_OTHER_WRITABLE	30;42	# dir that is sticky and other-writable (+t,o+w)
OTHER_WRITABLE	34;42	# dir that is other-writable (o+w) and not sticky
STICKY	37;44	# dir with the sticky bit set (+t) and not other-writable

# This is for files with execute permission:
EXEC	92

# List any file extensions like '.gz' or '.tar' that you would like ls
# to colorize below. Put the extension, a space, and the color init string.
# (and any comments you want to add after a '#')

# Or if you want to colorize scripts even if they do not have the
# executable bit actually set.
#.sh	92
#.csh	92

# editor temp files (dim)
*.swp	2
*.swo	2
*.sw?	2
*~	2
# build artefacts
.o	38;5;127
.a	38;5;127

